calc -> compute-tax everywhere, move a fn to another namespace — with zero behavior change\n  - outputs are typically a set of coordinated edits across all referencing files + evidence that no call site was missed and behavior is unchanged\n\nStrengths (proven traits — these patterns have been observed to work; mimic where they fit, adapt as needed):\n  - **Trait:** ENUMERATE every reference before editing — find all call sites (definition, callers, tests, docs) so the rename/move is exhaustive; a missed reference is a compile/runtime break (confidence 0.70, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: the symbol is referenced from multiple files including tests\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:sequence [:code {:reads [:symbol :repo] :writes [:references] :fn (fn [{:keys [inputs]}] {:references :all-call-sites})}] [:map-each {:items :references :as :ref :writes [:edits]} [:llm {:reads [:ref :new-name] :writes [:edit]}]] [:final {:keys [:edits]}]]\n      ```\n\n  - **Trait:** gate the change on a compile/test run after editing — a behavior-preserving rename/move is correct ONLY if everything still resolves and the existing tests stay green (confidence 0.70, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: a build or test command exists to confirm exhaustiveness and behavior preservation\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:sequence [:llm {:writes [:edits]}] [:code {:reads [:edits] :writes [:build-result] :fn (fn [{:keys [inputs]}] {:build-result :compiles-tests-green})}] [:final {:keys [:edits :build-result]}]]\n      ```\n\nWeaknesses (observed failure modes — avoid these patterns, apply the recommended fix where applicable):\n  - **Failure mode:** editing only the definition and the obvious callers (a code-building instinct that stops at the first file) leaves stale references in tests/docs/secondary namespaces — the rename is half-applied and the build breaks (confidence 0.70, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: the symbol is referenced beyond the file that defines it\n    - Recommended fix: first enumerate ALL references repo-wide (grep/AST), then map-each an edit over every site, then compile to confirm none was missed\n\n  - **Failure mode:** sneaking a behavior change into a rename/move (renaming AND tweaking logic) destroys the refactor's safety — reviewers can no longer trust it as a no-op and regressions hide in the diff (confidence 0.70, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: the task is strictly a rename/move with behavior preserved\n    - Recommended fix: keep the change behavior-identical; if logic must change too, do it as a separate, reviewable step after the rename lands green\n\nRepresentative uses (concrete tasks this pattern has shipped on):\n  - rename the function calc to compute-tax across the billing namespace and update every caller, including the test files\n  - move a shared helper from the request handler into a pure pricing namespace and repoint every import\n\n   Adopt / adapt / SPECIALIZE / mint-adjacent — use the evidence above, not the score alone:\n   - ADOPT this pattern as-is if it is an EXACT fit for your task.\n   - ADAPT it (keep the shape, override the specifics) if it mostly fits.\n   - SPECIALIZE — if this is a BROAD fit rather than an exact one (its `:summary`/strengths describe a more general shape than your task, or its `:avoid-when` flags your context), mint a domain-specialized CHILD of THIS behavior. The child keeps the parent's proven shape but pins your domain, gets a STABLE derived id, and ACCRUES evidence under the parent (it is retrievable on subsequent classify-behaviors calls):\n\n       (mint-behavior! \"<short-kebab-name>\"\n                       {:capabilities [\"<concrete action verb + object>\"]\n                        :strengths [{:trait \"<what works AND why>\"\n                                     :good-when \"<context guard>\"\n                                     :reco